    "params": {
        "video_subject": "1 · The price tag you can taste",
        "video_script": "Number one: the price tag you can taste. Researchers at Caltech gave volunteers sips of wine while they lay inside a brain scanner. They were told they would taste five different Cabernet Sauvignons, and they saw the price of each one. But there were really only three wines. Two of them came back twice: once with a cheap price, once with an expensive one. A wine labelled forty-five dollars was the same wine they had also tasted labelled five dollars. You can guess the ratings. People said the expensive version tasted better. But here's what surprised the researchers: it wasn't just talk. When people believed the wine was expensive, a brain area linked to pleasure became more active. Same liquid, same tongue, a different experience in the brain. And the basic taste areas of the brain didn't change. The difference was in how much they enjoyed it. Your brain doesn't just measure what's in the glass. It predicts how good something will be, and price is one of its strongest clues. Then it partly delivers the experience it predicted. That's why the expensive version of almost anything can feel better, even when it isn't. Try this today: next time you compare two versions of something, cover the price first. Decide which you prefer, then look. But price isn't the only thing that makes something feel valuable. Sometimes all it takes is having less of it.",
